<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alba Fucens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">303 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alba Fucens was a Roman colony in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founded in 303 BCE on land taken from the Aequi near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fucine-lake">Fucine Lake</a>. Settled with 6,000 colonists, it held a strategic position on the later Via Valeria. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> used it to hold captive kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a>, who died there. Its excavated ruins lie near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a> d'Albe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alba-fucens.jpg" alt="Alba Fucens" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PaestumPaestum · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Remains_of_Alba_Fucens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
